1
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:08,000
I am Anabel González,
a psychiatrist from A Coruña.

2
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,000
I work in public health

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,440
and have written some
popular books.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,480
The last one isWhere does it come from?,

5
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,280
a book that focuses,
above all, on fear

6
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,960
and how to develop the security
that can help us with it.

7
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:25,760
Hello, Annabelle.

8
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:27,400
I am Rafael Fernández Campos,

9
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:32,040
doctor in Economics and Public Finance
from the Autonomous University of Madrid.

10
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:33,920
Professionally, I work with data.

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,960
But the real reason I
wanted to be here with you today

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,960
is because I am a father of three children.

13
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:41,200
When you become a parent, it

14
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:45,240
seems like fear begins
to become part of your life.

15
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,960
Yes, having children is a
more complicated career than the other one, right?

16
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,480
-I think so.
-Yes.

17
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:55,080
Many
very natural fears arise with children

18
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,720
because the child needs to be with us

19
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:59,520
to feel protected

20
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,800
and we are the ones who should be afraid
for them,

21
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:05,440
but not so much fear that it blocks us

22
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:09,520
and prevents us from enjoying ourselves
and having good times.

23
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:13,480
Anabel, you are a psychotherapist.

24
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:16,440
You are a doctor of medicine.  You are a psychiatrist.

25
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,040
You've written several books,

26
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:21,640
but there's one in particular
that deals with fear.

27
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,000
I would like you to explain to me

28
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:26,880
how
these fear mechanisms work.

29
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:32,560
Fear is part
of our basic survival pack.

30
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:36,560
There are many animals
that have these kinds of emotions,

31
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,160
and human beings, without fear,

32
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:40,080
would have become extinct a long time ago.  This is what

33
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:43,160
makes

34
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,640
the body start moving if we face danger:
"We start the car engine

35
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:50,120
in case we have to run away."  What

36
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:55,400
happens is that, later, fear,
which is functional, good and necessary,

37
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,160
can become complicated for us,

38
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:59,880
precisely because of the things
our brain does

39
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,400
to try to do its job.

40
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,160
For example,
how do I know that something is dangerous?

41
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,080
At first, new things will scare
me just in case,

42
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,000
and the brain sometimes goes
a bit overboard with the "just in case" thing.

43
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:15,120
Then, everything
around me that scared me

44
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:17,320
will start to scare me.

45
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:22,480
I was bitten by a dog and I
was in an area of trees.

46
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,680
Now, when I get close to the trees, I'll say,
"What if a dog comes?",

47
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:28,280
and before I didn't think about it.

48
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:31,680
Then,
fear becomes associated with other things

49
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:35,800
and the brain doesn't do it because it is, it does it so
that we are prepared.  What

50
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:39,400
happens is that sometimes
we prepare a little too much.

51
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:42,240
Then, that's when

52
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,720
all this stuff we call anxiety,
panic... starts to happen,

53
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,320
which is when things get out of hand.

54
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:50,600
Understood, understood.

55
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:51,960
With this anxiety thing, it

56
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:56,240
seems like we have an epidemic now,
no matter the age.

57
00:02:56,680 --> 00:03:00,040
You, in one of your books,
call anxiety

58
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:02,240
"the fear that sticks with you."

59
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:05,640
How did you find this happy phrase?

60
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:07,640
Well, that's a bit of it.
Fear sticks

61
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,600
to what is
around what is truly dangerous.

62
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:15,000
And anxiety would be that I
even think about things that could happen,

63
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,040
because anxiety, when it gets out of hand,

64
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,000
already makes you think

65
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:20,440
about the worst possibilities
that could happen

66
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:23,800
and, in addition,
you get anxious as if they were inevitable

67
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:25,800
and as if you couldn't do anything.

68
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:28,680
Then, there's a ladder,

69
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:31,080
more fear, more catastrophic thoughts,

70
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:32,480
and you end up in panic.

71
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:35,560
And here you can't think
about anything at all.

72
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:38,480
Here, the part of the brain that thinks,
which is what we have here, is

73
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:41,200
taken out of the equation.  We

74
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:43,480
can also reverse this.

75
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:48,320
There are therapies that work
to untie the knot that has formed.

76
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:53,200
There is an experiment
to manufacture a phobia,

77
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:55,120
which is an experiment
that today would not be ethical

78
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,960
or approved, by Watson,

79
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:01,160
who made a child
have a phobia of mice.

80
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,800
The boy liked mice, they
were funny to him,

81
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:07,240
but they started making a sudden noise
every time a mouse appeared

82
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,640
and the boy ended up being
afraid of mice,

83
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,800
which, in principle, he didn't have any.

84
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:13,480
I think they
didn't take it out on the poor guy afterwards, he

85
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,240
was left
with a fear of mice.

86
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:19,600
How does this translate
into what can happen to any of us?

87
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:22,600
Sometimes, there
's something there that's neutral,

88
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:24,880
that's harmless,
that's not a problem,

89
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:28,480
but something
scary happens to us next to that

90
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:33,240
and the brain says,
"That was here, I'll keep it.

91
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,880
That's scary too."

92
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,400
And this, which is not dangerous,
may continue to scare me,

93
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:40,360
perhaps, for the rest of my life,

94
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:42,520
and condition me when it comes to doing things.

95
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:45,560
And when I start to function like this,

96
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,320
sometimes
this can also become generalized.

97
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:51,080
Then, I
became increasingly afraid of more and more things.

98
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:52,480
In fact, there are people

99
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,280
who acquire a phobia
of leaving the house,

100
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,720
people who
literally stay locked in their homes

101
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:01,800
without any kind of lock
to prevent them from doing so

102
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:04,560
because everything,
absolutely everything, ends up scaring them.

103
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:06,280
It's something we call "agoraphobia."

104
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:08,720
And you say that there are therapies
that can unravel

105
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:09,800
this tangle?

106
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:14,720
Of course, because one of the mechanisms
that makes things worse,

107
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,000
not that it generates it,
but that it does make it worse,

108
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:19,320
is avoiding what scares us.

109
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,040
And it's logical, right?

110
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,680
If something has been dangerous, you say:
"I won't do it again."

111
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:29,320
But, if I don't do it again,
I'm more afraid of it.

112
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:32,640
And if it turns out that it's something I do have to
do...

113
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:34,360
I'm afraid to take the car

114
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:36,040
because I had a traffic accident,

115
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:37,560
but I have to take the car.

116
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:38,800
Then, I do everything:

117
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:41,680
I go with a friend, I take the bus...

118
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:43,880
But if one day I have to take the car,

119
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:46,440
then I will be a thousand times more afraid of it

120
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:48,800
because the fear has been eating away at me

121
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:50,520
and has been growing.

122
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,680
Then, some of the therapies
that work with this are getting

123
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:55,880
out of hand.

124
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,720
Sometimes, to get out of a maze,

125
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:03,960
the best way out is to
undo the path we have taken

126
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:06,680
to get to
where we are.  What

127
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:08,640
happens is that it's very lazy

128
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:11,520
because, once you've avoided a lot of things

129
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,800
and are so scared, it's

130
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:20,040
very difficult for you to
regain lost ground.

131
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,320
When you talk about trauma,
the fear of the past comes to mind.

132
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,960
Is it possible to be afraid of the past?

133
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:29,760
How can we face

134
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:32,960
the traumatic situations
we have suffered?

135
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:35,840
Sometimes, the past is hard

136
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:39,400
and we have survived
by never looking back.

137
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:43,080
We say,
"I'm moving forward and this has already happened,"

138
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,280
and it's like closing a chapter.

139
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:48,240
But, really,
if the things that have happened

140
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:50,520
still produce feelings in us,

141
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:53,520
it is because something has been blocked.

142
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:57,800
And these memories can influence us now
in many things.

143
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:01,240
I may be having a problem
with a coworker right now

144
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:04,960
because, perhaps,
I have had a similar experience

145
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:08,280
with a person in the past
that has nothing to do with this one,

146
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,640
and I am now unable to resolve it.

147
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:13,440
Then, when this happens,

148
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,800
we have to go back

149
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:17,560
because, if we don't untie this knot,

150
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:21,080
everything that happens
afterwards will get stuck too.

151
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,680
I work a lot with EMDR,
which is a type of trauma therapy,

152
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:26,960
and in it what is done

153
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:29,680
is to look for, precisely,
what the roots are.

154
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:31,760
I have a problem now.  I

155
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,480
may have a problem, for example,
in relationships.

156
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:39,680
Then,
we will look for what this is connected to.

157
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:43,400
The problem I have
is that I always end up in relationships

158
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:46,040
where I have to do what the other person wants,

159
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:50,240
and I, in the end,
end up giving up what I need.

160
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:53,440
We will go to the first relationships,

161
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:56,360
to see if
this pattern was created in these first relationships.

162
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:58,880
And then also to the first bonds,

163
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:01,560
which here we return
to childhood, to parents,

164
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:04,440
which is where we learn
to bond and connect.

165
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:07,400
My parents may have
loved me very much,

166
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:10,640
but I grew up
with a mother who was very sick

167
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:14,760
and I had to worry
about her more than they,

168
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:16,720
with the time they had
or the energy they had,

169
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:17,880
could worry about me.

170
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:22,000
So I got used
to giving up what I need

171
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:23,880
and I fit perfectly into couples

172
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:26,120
where I end up doing the same.

173
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:28,440
Then, what we would do would be,

174
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:31,880
these experiences
and the specific memories

175
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:35,240
in which
this problem has been formed,

176
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:39,040
we work on them one by one
and unblock them.

177
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:43,960
EMDR is called this because it uses,
among its ingredients,

178
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:46,800
systems to unlock memories.

179
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:48,640
One is eye movement.

180
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:52,920
The therapist would make this movement,
the patient follows the movement

181
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,760
and, by thinking about a traumatic memory
and doing this for a while,

182
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:00,120
of course, it
is a much more complex process, you

183
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:02,640
have to ask a series of things

184
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:05,200
and the therapist
has to help at certain times,

185
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:07,240
the memory loses strength.

186
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:10,360
If I have a memory of an accident,
for example,

187
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:12,960
years have passed and when I think about it it

188
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:16,800
still has
a very strong impact on me and I get anxious,

189
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:18,880
by doing this,

190
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,600
what I will notice when I return to the memory

191
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:25,760
is that the memory
becomes less intense.

192
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:28,880
And after a while,
every time I go back,

193
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:30,960
the feeling changes,

194
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:33,480
I can even see the most distant memory.

195
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:35,720
When something
has been engraved on us,

196
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:39,400
we are left with a very fixed image,
as it fades and there comes a time when

197
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:41,800
the memory loses all its strength

198
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,080
and, sometimes, it
can even be difficult to remember it.

199
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:49,800
Most of the time
we don't have such a clear memory.

200
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:52,480
Then,
when working on memories,

201
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:57,000
both of things that happened
and of what is activating us now,

202
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:59,880
because, perhaps, I had the problem
of the car accident

203
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:02,760
and now I get into it
and every time I do it I am afraid

204
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:05,680
or just think about getting into the car
and I am already afraid, we

205
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:09,600
also work
with the present and the future.

206
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:15,080
In this way,
the problems are cut from the root,

207
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,120
from all the experiences

208
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:19,240
that have to do
with the relationship problem

209
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:20,360
that I am having,

210
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:24,240
with the fear I have of driving
or with the problem that I have now.  What

211
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:30,320
is complex about this type
of treatment is finding where the root is.

212
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:34,480
Therefore, an exercise
that I sometimes propose is to look for,

213
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:35,640
when we were talking about fear,

214
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:39,000
what
our first experience of fear is,

215
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:41,320
what is the first memory

216
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:44,440
in which we remember being afraid.

217
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:48,080
And the second question would be:
"And am I still afraid?".

218
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:51,960
But it's not that I care much about it,
but when I think about it, it

219
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:55,200
's not a completely neutral memory.

220
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:58,880
If I stop for a while,
is the memory uncomfortable?

221
00:10:58,960 --> 00:11:02,960
This would be an example of a memory
that is not processed.

222
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:08,840
And, by working on this memory, we
would begin to dry up the root of fear.

223
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,720
So now I could put myself

224
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,920
in the situations that are scaring me

225
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:16,400
with a much more proportionate fear.

226
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:19,880
Otherwise, sometimes,
I have a situation now

227
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:21,240
that scares me,

228
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:22,880
not so much because of the situation,

229
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:25,800
but because of the whole chain of things

230
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:28,960
that have been leaving
a little fear in my body.

231
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:30,800
So, now I have a fear

232
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:33,000
that I do
n't think is that big of a deal.

233
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:34,600
Why am I acting like this,

234
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:37,000
if what's happening
isn't of this caliber?

235
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:39,560
But still,
if I look back and link everything together,

236
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:44,200
all of this set of things
does explain the fear I have.  It

237
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:48,400
is proportionate to all of this,
not proportionate to this.

238
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,840
So, trauma therapies
work a bit like this.

239
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:55,800
In a way,
this is likeresetting yourbrain.

240
00:11:56,360 --> 00:12:00,160
We have fears that are stuck,
that are blocked.

241
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:02,640
None of these fears are functional.

242
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:06,120
Functional fear is what I feel now,
when I have the lion in front of me.

243
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:06,880
It's this one.

244
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:11,520
But the lion I had ten years ago
no longer makes sense to continue here,

245
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:15,880
so what we do
is undo that influence.

246
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:20,480
The brain resetseverything

247
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:25,320
and puts it back in its place
as just another memory.

248
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,360
Many things
that used to scare us no longer scare us

249
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:30,240
because the brain has done this on its own.

250
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:33,200
Just as there are things
that disgusted us

251
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:35,800
at a certain time,
now, with the passage of time, they

252
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:38,880
no longer produce any sensation in us.

253
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,200
When this doesn't happen with a memory,

254
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:44,320
that memory
needs to be worked on.

255
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:47,400
And this is
one of the things we can do.  It

256
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:50,800
also helps, sometimes, to talk about it,
discuss it with other people,

257
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:52,800
listen to other perspectives...

258
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:56,800
But, in my experience,
EMDR works even with things

259
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:59,520
that we have talked about, that we have thought about
,

260
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:03,880
but this blockage is on a
more emotional, deeper level,

261
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:07,200
and this unlocks it
in a different way.

262
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,560
When we also talked about avoidance,

263
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:15,160
there are other types of trauma therapies
that work in a different way.

264
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:18,240
For example, what
exposure therapy does is:

265
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:21,960
this scares me,
and instead of avoiding it

266
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:24,640
and stopping doing things that trigger it, I

267
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:25,600
stay here.

268
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:29,320
So, at first,
I'm really scared, but I'm still here.

269
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:32,880
And then it scares me less,
and there comes a time when I get used to it.

270
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:35,440
It's like, "I'm afraid of the car.

271
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:38,400
I'll take it every day
until I get used to it again."

272
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:39,840
This is how fear can go away.

273
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:42,560
Then, in exposure therapy,

274
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:44,880
the person is in contact

275
00:13:44,960 --> 00:13:48,000
with that difficult emotion

276
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:49,840
long enough for it to subside.

277
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:54,160
In EMDR, what is done is
that we connect with this situation,

278
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:57,600
and the person unlocks it
with eye movement

279
00:13:57,680 --> 00:13:59,000
and things come to them,

280
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:00,920
things that may have to do with this

281
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:03,240
or may have to do
with these connections,

282
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:05,120
with these roots that we were talking about.

283
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:07,000
And, from time to time, we come back here,

284
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:09,160
just to do it again,

285
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:12,600
until all of this connects,

286
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:16,040
and here this gets smaller and smaller

287
00:14:16,120 --> 00:14:17,480
and there's nothing left.

288
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:21,080
Later, I will
remember this situation again,

289
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:24,800
but it will be another experience.  It

290
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:30,200
will be part of my life wisdom,
my normal way of functioning.

291
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:32,800
My mother, it must be a good task.  I

292
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:36,840
understand that, in addition,
sometimes you refer to the blockages

293
00:14:36,920 --> 00:14:39,680
and paralysis caused by fear.

294
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:42,560
In other words, it becomes a situation
of true paralysis.

295
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:44,640
How does this mechanism work?

296
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:48,040
How can it be untied and untangled?

297
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:50,680
When I was writing the book,
I asked a question

298
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:54,240
on social media
about what people associated with fear,

299
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:56,440
because fear itself is not a problem.

300
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:58,960
In fact, the other day someone told me:

301
00:14:59,040 --> 00:15:00,000
"I'm never afraid."

302
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:02,240
Sorry, I'm afraid, everyone here has them, it

303
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:03,960
's impossible for you not to have them.  What

304
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:06,160
happens is
that we only call it "fear"

305
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:08,640
when fear blocks us,
when it gets us stuck,

306
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:11,720
and these are already associated problems.

307
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:16,560
So, this paralysis thing really
is a survival mechanism,

308
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:19,360
even if it doesn't seem like it,
but it can bother us quite a bit.

309
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:23,480
For example, when we
have a dangerous situation,

310
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:26,760
the car starts up in
case we have to run away,

311
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:28,360
in case we have to do something,

312
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:31,240
and the first responses are
active responses.  What

313
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:34,360
is logical is to fight
and, if I can't fight, I run away.

314
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:37,960
But if I can't fight
because the other person is bigger

315
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:39,800
and I can't run away because there's no way out,

316
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:42,240
and this happens a lot,
for example, when we're children,

317
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:46,840
because we can't face it,
run away, let's see where we go...,

318
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:50,520
the best solution is to stay still

319
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:53,320
because, if not, more will fall on you.

320
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:57,680
And if you see that staying still,
which would be paralysis, is

321
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:01,280
not getting you anywhere,
the body starts to say:

322
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,240
"I'll lower the revs. There

323
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:07,640
's no point in having the engine running
at full speed if there's nowhere to go,"

324
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,080
so we bow our heads, we submit...

326
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:18,960
You say, "Yes, yes"
to stop them from doing it again.

327
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:21,320
And the emergency response

328
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:23,960
is to collapse.

329
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:27,880
This is also
a survival mechanism.

330
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:30,880
In fact, if you are a herbivore, for example,

331
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:32,440
and a predator catches you,

332
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:35,840
the best thing you can do
is fall down

333
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:38,640
because then
the predator will think you are dead,

334
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:41,080
and a predator does
not eat dead prey.  It

335
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:42,680
is also survival for them.  They

336
00:16:42,760 --> 00:16:45,320
could be eating
an animal in poor condition

337
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:48,000
or something dangerous to them.

338
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:50,200
So, if the animal is in a state

339
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:51,880
where it appears to be dead, it

340
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:53,320
survives.

341
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:58,280
Some authors call it a "defensive cascade",

342
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:01,160
and it is a response that is initially activated,

343
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:03,160
but when it sees
that there is no possibility,

344
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:05,440
it remains still and then goes down.

345
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:08,200
People, for example,
who suffer an assault, there

346
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:11,520
was a lot of talk
about sexual assaults,

347
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:16,400
believe that the only answer
is to be brave and fight.

348
00:17:16,480 --> 00:17:17,760
And fighting is senseless

349
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:20,120
when you have no
chance of winning.

350
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:23,440
Becoming paralyzed
is a way to protect yourself.  Falling

351
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:25,800
down
is a way to protect yourself.

352
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:29,120
It is found in the survival instinct
of our species.

353
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:32,640
And this response of paralysis

354
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:35,160
or submission or collapse,

355
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:37,640
which are passive responses,
emergency responses,

356
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:41,200
those when there is nothing to do,
if they are repeated many times... We

357
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:42,360
said that children

359
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:47,960
Another response
a child has is to cry.

360
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:52,840
But if you're in a family
that doesn't meet your needs, you

361
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:54,400
end up not even crying.

362
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,280
A girl
who spoke on social media

363
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:01,760
about her experience
in an orphanage in Eastern countries explained

364
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:03,720
how children don't cry there, they

365
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:05,880
don't cry because why?

366
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:09,800
So, when you have lived
in a very hostile environment,

367
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:14,080
where there is no fighting,
no escaping, why cry?, you are

368
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:16,400
only left with the other answers.

369
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:18,400
And they are triggered so many times

370
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:20,480
that later, when you become an adult,

371
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:24,280
or if you have been in an abusive relationship,
for example,

372
00:18:24,360 --> 00:18:27,720
or in a
very problematic situation for a long time, you

373
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:30,520
no longer know how to react
in any other way.

374
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:33,000
When you could fight, you don't fight anymore.

375
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:36,480
When you could walk away
from a problematic situation, you do

376
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:37,560
n't.  We

377
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:39,360
see this many times

378
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:41,840
in situations
of domestic violence,

379
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:45,560
which are people who, perhaps,
in their childhood stories, have

380
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:47,680
had very complicated childhoods,

381
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:50,880
and when they find themselves
in an abusive relationship, they

382
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:52,720
don't get out of it.

383
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:57,240
People sometimes look at them and say:
"But why didn't you leave earlier?".

384
00:18:57,560 --> 00:18:59,960
If you've been blocked since childhood,

385
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:03,400
leaving is not easy at all.

386
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:06,240
The body knows how to do it,

387
00:19:06,320 --> 00:19:08,000
but it's as if you've forgotten.

388
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:12,640
You need to remember again
how to get out of a situation,

389
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:16,880
that you can leave and that you can, sometimes,
fight for what you need.

390
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:21,840
And when we talk about fear
and the situations that scare us,

391
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:27,040
perhaps a very common
and very normal strategy would be to try to avoid them.

392
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:29,200
That is, not confronting them.  Do you

393
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:31,920
think these
fear avoidance strategies

394
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,840
are good, are they healthy
or can they have consequences later?

395
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:35,760
How do you see it?

396
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:37,320
To avoid it would be to escape.

397
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:41,120
If you are, for example,
with a partner who doesn't treat you well,

398
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:43,840
avoiding this relationship
would be the best thing you can do.

399
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:46,360
The problem is when we avoid things...

400
00:19:46,440 --> 00:19:49,200
Sometimes, we avoid things
that are good for us.

401
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:51,040
Or we avoid everything,

402
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:53,320
and this starts
to make our lives very complicated.

403
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:55,680
All the mechanisms that the brain has,

404
00:19:55,760 --> 00:20:00,280
including what happens in phobias,
are fundamentally something that makes sense.

405
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:02,600
It's the brain
putting itself in "just in case,"

406
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:05,920
it's the brain associating things
that could be dangerous.  What

407
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:10,960
happens is that,
sometimes, it slips out of our hands

408
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:13,320
or sometimes it appears where it doesn't belong

409
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:16,160
or stops being useful.

410
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:20,440
But everything our mind does is
for a reason.

411
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:23,680
Sometimes,
when we go back to the beginning,

412
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:25,760
if I think: "I'm paralyzed,

413
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:28,520
why am I paralyzed
at work with colleagues

414
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,480
if I'm an adult,
I have resources?

415
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:33,600
In other situations, I've been
able to manage it,

416
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:34,840
and now why not?".

417
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:37,800
Sometimes, the answer lies further back.

418
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:39,040
If I think:

419
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:42,160
"When was the first time I had
this feeling?",

420
00:20:42,480 --> 00:20:45,560
things certainly
make a lot more sense here.

421
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:48,960
The thing is that this blockage has
stayed with me,

422
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:52,960
and when something happens
that the brain connects with it,

423
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:53,960
I get blocked again.

424
00:20:55,240 --> 00:20:57,560
And that's how the brain thinks
all the time.

425
00:20:57,640 --> 00:21:02,680
Me, if I'm in a meeting with you,
we're here talking,

426
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:05,680
our brains are connecting
with other conversations,

427
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:07,040
with other encounters..., we

428
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:10,000
won't be constantly reinventing ourselves.

429
00:21:10,080 --> 00:21:14,080
But when the brain connects
with a blocking situation,

430
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:16,680
then...

431
00:21:17,040 --> 00:21:18,440
If I reminded you of someone

432
00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:20,360
you had
a bad experience with,

433
00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:23,320
maybe right now you would be having
a strange feeling about me,

434
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:25,600
and maybe it would have nothing to do with me.

435
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:28,280
But the brain
is always associating things.  He

436
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:29,760
does it for our good.

437
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:32,080
But this
can sometimes backfire.

438
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:34,680
"The brain does it for our good."

439
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:36,640
I'll stick with this phrase, Anabel,

440
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:40,320
because, besides,
when you're next to someone

441
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:42,080
who happens to be afraid,

442
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:45,240
the phrase always comes out:
"Stay calm"

443
00:21:45,320 --> 00:21:47,400
or "Stay calm",
"Don't be afraid".

444
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:52,880
As if this phrase were magical
and could solve fear.

445
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:55,800
These kinds of mantras, phrases,

446
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:58,920
which often seem to
provoke even more reaction,

447
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:00,280
is this a good mechanism?

448
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:02,280
What should we do in these situations,

449
00:22:02,360 --> 00:22:04,480
when we are next to someone who
is suffering?

450
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:09,480
Of course, telling someone who has anxiety,

451
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,800
"Stay calm"

452
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:13,640
is like telling someone
who has a wound, "Don't bleed."

453
00:22:14,120 --> 00:22:15,800
It's exactly the same.

454
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:18,360
In fact, people who have anxiety,
when they are told this, get angry

455
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:20,720
because they say:
"Of course, I would like it.

456
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:23,640
That is exactly what
I would like at this moment."  It

457
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:25,880
is true that the way

458
00:22:25,960 --> 00:22:29,480
we talk
to ourselves has an influence.

459
00:22:30,040 --> 00:22:32,000
In fact, people who have anxiety

460
00:22:32,400 --> 00:22:36,200
say things to themselves
that make them even more afraid.

461
00:22:36,280 --> 00:22:39,240
But, because the person
is saying these things to themselves, what

462
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:44,920
they are told from the outside
doesn't get through.

463
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:47,920
That's why we need to
do double the work.

464
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,920
Learn to say things
to others that will help them.

465
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,560
"Calm down" when he's
in an anxiety attack, no,

466
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:54,880
but you can tell him,

467
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:58,120
"I'm here. Do you
need anything? Can I help you?",

468
00:22:58,480 --> 00:23:00,040
which won't make him feel bad

469
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:04,240
and we may not be able to perform miracles,
but it does help.

470
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:07,800
And then the same person
can learn

471
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:11,160
to see what things
can be said when it's wrong.

472
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:12,920
In the midst of a crisis it is difficult,

473
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,480
but afterwards
we can think a little.

474
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:20,280
Above all, more than whatever calms us down
at that moment,

475
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:24,560
which sometimes
means I need to see the doctor, have them

476
00:23:24,640 --> 00:23:25,800
give me something to lower it,

477
00:23:25,880 --> 00:23:27,880
and then we'll see
how we fix the issue,

478
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:31,840
work on developing,
above all,

479
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:36,640
the security that will make us
have less of this problem.

480
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:41,800
The most important thing is to develop
what could prevent this fear

481
00:23:41,880 --> 00:23:46,160
and what can even help us get
through situations

482
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:49,520
that can cause us fear,
which is to have a greater sense of security.

483
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:54,720
Confidence does
n't mean you keep saying,

484
00:23:54,800 --> 00:23:57,680
in a higher-than-normal tone of voice,

485
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:00,640
that you're very sure of what you're saying.
Quite the opposite.

486
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:03,400
Security comes from reflection,

487
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:06,880
from the ability to establish
relationships with others,

488
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:08,440
from trust...

489
00:24:09,320 --> 00:24:11,200
Calm is fleeting.

490
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,800
I can do something that calms me down
at a certain moment,

491
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:17,880
but if the fear returns
shortly after, what do I do?

492
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:23,120
People who are afraid
sometimes find small solutions

493
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:26,200
that work in the short term,

494
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:28,480
like avoidance, which you mentioned earlier.

495
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:31,960
If I say: "I'm not going to this place,
it bothers me a bit,"

496
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:34,520
then that's fine, but what if you have to go there?

497
00:24:34,600 --> 00:24:37,080
What if it
happens to you again later somewhere else?

498
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:40,800
There are things that can be avoided,

499
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:43,800
that solve the problem for the moment,
but that don't solve it later.

500
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:47,360
On the other hand, security is something
that we can gradually grow

502
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:52,800
until it helps us neutralize
fear a little

503
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:55,720
and, even if we are afraid, we

504
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:58,520
can do things.

505
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:01,240
There are things we do
even though they scare us

506
00:25:01,320 --> 00:25:04,840
because we feel
that internal sense of security.

507
00:25:05,640 --> 00:25:09,560
Security comes about
spontaneously

508
00:25:09,880 --> 00:25:14,560
when you have grown up with people who have
given you protection and care.

509
00:25:15,320 --> 00:25:16,920
And when I say this, I always want to insist

510
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,160
that we don't have to be perfect parents
and do everything right,

511
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:21,880
because that is horrible

512
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:24,720
for the safety of parents
and children.

513
00:25:25,040 --> 00:25:28,080
We have to do it as best we can, but be there.

514
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:33,600
If I have a problem at school
or something happens to me with a friend

515
00:25:33,880 --> 00:25:36,280
or I'm worried about
something that's happening at home

516
00:25:36,640 --> 00:25:37,880
and someone notices it,

517
00:25:37,960 --> 00:25:41,280
and I know that
if I have a problem, I can explain it,

518
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:44,800
I feel more confident, I

519
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:46,440
feel more secure,

520
00:25:46,720 --> 00:25:49,880
I will feel that I can trust people.

521
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:53,840
Thanks to this,
if difficult things happen, I

522
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:57,560
won't be knocked down so easily.  We

523
00:25:57,640 --> 00:25:58,800
all have a limit

524
00:25:58,880 --> 00:26:00,800
and sometimes life
gets very complicated,

525
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:04,360
but I have more capacity
to deal with things.

526
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:06,240
Sometimes, life is very scary

527
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:11,240
and you need to have that feeling of
security to get through it.

528
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:12,120
Yes, yes.

529
00:26:12,200 --> 00:26:14,800
You said a phrase earlier
that drove me crazy, that

530
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:16,880
"calm is fleeting."

531
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:19,640
So, if the calm is fleeting,

532
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:22,320
that means we will go through
stormy times.

533
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:23,680
Yes.  Is there

534
00:26:24,360 --> 00:26:29,000
any way we can guarantee

535
00:26:29,080 --> 00:26:33,080
or at least reasonably mitigate
these moments of anguish

536
00:26:33,160 --> 00:26:36,800
and be able to have that security,
that confidence that you mention?

537
00:26:36,880 --> 00:26:40,840
Sure, but how does
a sailor know he's safe?

538
00:26:40,920 --> 00:26:43,560
Because he trusts
his abilities to do it,

539
00:26:43,640 --> 00:26:47,400
because he has sailed many seas
of many different types, he knows

540
00:26:47,480 --> 00:26:50,440
when to enter them and when not to, he

541
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:52,880
knows when to return to port,
which is also something that needs to be known.  He has

542
00:26:53,280 --> 00:26:55,800
already been through a few storms,

543
00:26:55,880 --> 00:26:59,560
learned from each one
and continues to go sailing.

544
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:03,800
If we keep doing things, from every mistake,

545
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:06,880
from every thing that goes wrong,
we learn,

546
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:10,880
and that is what makes us
feel that we have resources.  It's

547
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:14,880
not that we don't have to be afraid of anything.

548
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:18,120
If there is a huge storm, we should
not go out to sea.

549
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:19,600
It's very clear.

550
00:27:19,960 --> 00:27:24,600
But I can feel
that I can handle many situations,

551
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:27,720
that I can navigate seas of different kinds.

552
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:32,320
And I feel like I can because I have
this feeling of security.

553
00:27:33,600 --> 00:27:37,800
Anabel, if the antidote
to fear is security,

554
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:40,240
can we grow security?

555
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:42,440
Is there any way to make it grow?

556
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:45,360
Yes, but little by little.

557
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:47,720
Security must be sown, it

558
00:27:47,800 --> 00:27:49,600
is something that grows little by little

559
00:27:49,680 --> 00:27:52,120
and that has a lot to do
not only with us,

560
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:54,880
but it has a lot to do
with relationships.

561
00:27:55,480 --> 00:27:57,880
Sometimes
we try to solve a problem

562
00:27:57,960 --> 00:27:59,920
right where the problem is.  Let

563
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:02,120
's try...

564
00:28:02,200 --> 00:28:04,680
I have a fever, I'll take something
to bring down my fever.

565
00:28:04,760 --> 00:28:08,720
It's okay, it has to be done.
But what caused my fever?

566
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:11,120
Maybe it's an infection,
I need to take an antibiotic,

567
00:28:11,200 --> 00:28:13,800
but I'm getting a lot of infections.

568
00:28:13,880 --> 00:28:16,080
Shouldn't I strengthen my
immune system?

569
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:17,760
Growing security

570
00:28:17,840 --> 00:28:21,280
is like strengthening
the emotional immune system

571
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:25,560
and is not so directly related
to what scares us, it

572
00:28:25,640 --> 00:28:27,080
is background work.

573
00:28:27,400 --> 00:28:29,240
For example, we said reflection.

574
00:28:29,560 --> 00:28:33,880
Reflection is like state-of-the-art software

575
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:37,880
that makes everything that happens,
whether it goes well or badly, give

576
00:28:37,960 --> 00:28:42,240
me more and more tools,
more and more resources.

577
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:44,160
I understand things better and better.  There

578
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:45,600
are still many things I don't understand, I

579
00:28:45,680 --> 00:28:48,800
ask myself many questions
and feel very curious.

580
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:51,800
Curiosity
also increases security.

581
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:54,040
What Murakami said:

582
00:28:54,440 --> 00:28:59,280
"Safety has a lot to do
with curiosity and courage."

583
00:28:59,360 --> 00:29:03,880
He was talking, specifically, about curiosity
and courage, which went together.

584
00:29:03,960 --> 00:29:08,040
Really, if you think about it,
a child feels curious

585
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:10,440
and this moves them
to do things that scare them,

586
00:29:10,520 --> 00:29:13,920
but, because they are more curious
than afraid, they manage to do it.

587
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:17,920
Another thing that also increases
security is trust.

588
00:29:18,800 --> 00:29:22,040
And this is complicated
because if we come, for example,

589
00:29:22,120 --> 00:29:25,760
from stories of betrayal or stories
of very difficult relationships, let's

590
00:29:26,360 --> 00:29:29,360
see who the handsome one is who trusts.  It

591
00:29:29,440 --> 00:29:32,280
's not easy,
but you need to regain your confidence

592
00:29:32,360 --> 00:29:33,880
because, if not, there's no chance.

593
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:35,800
A person who distrusts

594
00:29:35,880 --> 00:29:37,840
is a person
who is placed inside a bunker

595
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:39,520
and, inside that bunker, you

596
00:29:40,880 --> 00:29:42,520
may think that they won't hurt you,

597
00:29:42,600 --> 00:29:45,960
but inside you ca
n't really feel safe.  We

598
00:29:46,440 --> 00:29:49,240
feel safe when we walk down the street

599
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:52,760
and approach people,
we understand them,

600
00:29:52,840 --> 00:29:56,680
when we connect with someone,
when we learn about things...

601
00:29:56,760 --> 00:29:59,960
We need to explore
to feel safe.

602
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:02,320
How will we feel safe driving

603
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:05,040
if we always have our car in the parking lot?  It ca

604
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:06,880
n't be.

605
00:30:07,160 --> 00:30:10,400
Then, trust, curiosity,

606
00:30:10,920 --> 00:30:13,880
reflection, exploration

607
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:16,080
and looking inside ourselves too,

608
00:30:16,160 --> 00:30:19,200
because exploration is not only
about what happens outside,

609
00:30:19,280 --> 00:30:21,880
but also about what
happens inside us

610
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:24,240
and what happens inside the rest.

611
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:27,560
A very interesting thing
to do to be able to reflect better

612
00:30:27,640 --> 00:30:28,480
is to listen.  It

613
00:30:29,080 --> 00:30:33,280
's not a very common sport, is it?

614
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:37,880
Because, sometimes, what
we do is have monologues for two.

615
00:30:38,200 --> 00:30:40,560
Or, sometimes, when we talk to someone,

616
00:30:40,640 --> 00:30:42,880
we are more busy
preparing an answer

617
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:45,160
to win the argument

618
00:30:45,560 --> 00:30:49,360
than understanding
exactly what the other person is like.  We

619
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:52,080
could say
that a very convenient sport

620
00:30:52,160 --> 00:30:56,320
would be to try to truly understand,
with true curiosity,

621
00:30:57,240 --> 00:31:00,760
how someone
completely different from us thinks,

622
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:03,800
someone with whom we share
absolutely nothing.

623
00:31:03,880 --> 00:31:05,920
If we are able to feel curious

624
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:08,960
about understanding someone completely different, we will

625
00:31:09,320 --> 00:31:10,800
probably become more confident.

626
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:12,880
It is a highly recommended sport.

627
00:31:13,240 --> 00:31:14,160
Oh, how good.  What you

628
00:31:14,960 --> 00:31:17,800
're saying,
if I understand you correctly,

629
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:20,640
is that security
depends more on us

630
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:25,480
than on controlling our environment,
which, in the end, is uncontrollable.

631
00:31:26,160 --> 00:31:28,840
But now let's get back to being a father.

632
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:31,400
Those of us who are parents and have children, our

633
00:31:31,480 --> 00:31:34,240
children, in the end,
are not as much within our control

634
00:31:34,320 --> 00:31:35,600
as we would like.

635
00:31:36,120 --> 00:31:39,600
So, how can we guarantee...?

636
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:41,240
Well, I don't know if the word is guarantee,

637
00:31:41,320 --> 00:31:43,000
but how can we help

638
00:31:43,080 --> 00:31:45,680
our children
gain that security?

639
00:31:45,760 --> 00:31:49,960
In other words, is there any way
that we can do things

640
00:31:50,040 --> 00:31:53,680
that help them have
all these tools

641
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:58,080
that will help them with this security,
with the calm that you mentioned before?

642
00:31:58,160 --> 00:32:00,920
Note that sometimes we confuse terms.  I

643
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:05,240
often say that control
is a substitute for security.

644
00:32:05,880 --> 00:32:06,880
A substitute...

645
00:32:07,560 --> 00:32:10,120
Let's see, it
's not the worst thing we can have,

646
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:11,800
but it's not very good either

647
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:13,280
because, when unforeseen events occur,

648
00:32:13,360 --> 00:32:15,880
and life sometimes
gets a little original,

649
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:18,120
control is useless to us.

650
00:32:18,440 --> 00:32:21,800
People who function very much
through control

651
00:32:22,600 --> 00:32:25,320
need everything to be predictable.

652
00:32:25,640 --> 00:32:29,560
And, when they come across things
that are out of the ordinary, they are

653
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:31,800
disarmed

654
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:35,760
because they have no practice in improvising,
in being creative, in finding their own way in life,

655
00:32:35,840 --> 00:32:36,880
in experimenting... They have

656
00:32:37,720 --> 00:32:39,400
never done that.

657
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:41,600
We are always in control.

658
00:32:42,600 --> 00:32:45,400
Then,
raising children from a controlling perspective

659
00:32:46,120 --> 00:32:47,600
must be a nightmare

660
00:32:48,520 --> 00:32:51,040
because you will live in constant anxiety.

661
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:54,920
In addition, children,
each one of whom is different,

662
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:56,800
who go through many stages, who

663
00:32:56,880 --> 00:32:59,040
have to face
many difficulties... You

664
00:32:59,760 --> 00:33:02,160
have to go with the flow.

665
00:33:02,240 --> 00:33:03,800
Let's get back to sailing.  We

666
00:33:03,880 --> 00:33:05,920
don't sail by taking the helm and saying,

667
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:08,360
"This is how it will be,
regardless of how the sea gets."

668
00:33:08,440 --> 00:33:09,240
No.

669
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:15,080
We will have to maneuver
so that it can adapt.

670
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:18,600
And I don't think it's about
putting children in a bubble

671
00:33:18,680 --> 00:33:21,800
or exposing them,
as I think we're exposing them

672
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:23,640
to social media too early,

673
00:33:23,720 --> 00:33:26,120
or to things that they
're not ready to take on.  There

674
00:33:26,200 --> 00:33:28,560
must be something in the middle.

675
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:32,640
One thing that helps develop
security in children,

676
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:37,480
I'll use a psychological word that
we sometimes use,

677
00:33:37,560 --> 00:33:39,800
is the ability to mentalize the child.

678
00:33:39,880 --> 00:33:43,960
This means that I am able to see
that my son is not a clone of me

679
00:33:44,360 --> 00:33:47,600
who will do
everything I think he should do,

680
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:50,840
or who will be what I was not,
or any of that,

681
00:33:50,920 --> 00:33:54,000
but that he is a little person
who has his own mind

682
00:33:54,080 --> 00:33:57,480
and, even though he is very small,
he has a perspective on the world, he

683
00:33:57,560 --> 00:33:59,800
has a way of seeing things.

684
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:04,400
And I sense it, I understand it
and I think about what he thinks.

685
00:34:04,680 --> 00:34:07,080
If I have several children,
I know that they are different

686
00:34:07,160 --> 00:34:09,040
and each one experiences things
in their own way,

687
00:34:09,480 --> 00:34:14,160
and I can take into account their
personalities and abilities.

688
00:34:14,680 --> 00:34:16,600
If I'm able to look at my son like this,

689
00:34:17,160 --> 00:34:19,800
he'll be able to look at himself like this,

690
00:34:19,880 --> 00:34:22,360
he'll understand when things happen to him.

691
00:34:22,440 --> 00:34:24,760
Through the way
we look at them,

692
00:34:25,080 --> 00:34:27,680
they will be able to look at themselves.

693
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:30,560
And they will also learn
something very important,

694
00:34:30,640 --> 00:34:35,360
which is the ability to look at the other person
as a different person.

695
00:34:35,440 --> 00:34:38,760
So,
this ability to mentalize,

696
00:34:38,840 --> 00:34:40,680
to understand the child's mind,

697
00:34:40,760 --> 00:34:43,440
so that the child can learn
to also understand

698
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:46,960
how the minds of others work,
is a superpower.  It

699
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:50,680
is important
for a child to feel a sense of being

700
00:34:50,760 --> 00:34:53,920
loved unconditionally.

701
00:34:54,560 --> 00:34:56,640
That, whether he does well, whether he does badly,

702
00:34:56,720 --> 00:34:58,800
whether he achieves something,
whether he doesn't achieve something, he is

703
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:02,040
equally esteemed
and equally valuable.

704
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:05,000
And that, if he has a need, there

705
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:07,600
will be someone he can turn to,

706
00:35:07,680 --> 00:35:11,120
and that person will be there
consistently.

707
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:14,800
Not every day is perfect, in the same mood, with

708
00:35:14,880 --> 00:35:18,000
perfectly combed hair,
we are human beings and that is normal.

709
00:35:18,520 --> 00:35:20,880
But he will feel that he can trust

710
00:35:21,480 --> 00:35:24,040
that there is someone who values him for who he is,

711
00:35:24,120 --> 00:35:27,800
who asks him for things that are reasonable
for his age,

712
00:35:27,880 --> 00:35:29,880
because sometimes
we ask children for things

713
00:35:30,160 --> 00:35:31,800
that do not correspond to their age

714
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:34,920
or we do not give them
the responsibility

715
00:35:35,840 --> 00:35:38,480
that does come with their age.

716
00:35:38,560 --> 00:35:42,040
Look, a child who lives
with very worried parents

717
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:44,440
is sometimes unprotected

718
00:35:44,840 --> 00:35:46,720
because he sees his parents so worried

719
00:35:46,800 --> 00:35:49,960
that he doesn't dare
tell them worrying things

720
00:35:50,040 --> 00:35:51,480
so as not to worry them even more.

721
00:35:51,560 --> 00:35:53,960
These types of paradoxes occur.

722
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:59,000
Then, we need to feel
that our parents will be there

723
00:35:59,720 --> 00:36:01,120
no matter what

724
00:36:01,400 --> 00:36:05,480
and that, even if we do wrong, we will
not lose their affection.  They

725
00:36:05,560 --> 00:36:08,600
will correct us, they will tell us
that it is wrong, of course,

726
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:12,800
but that the affection is total
and guaranteed,

727
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:15,600
no matter what happens
and no matter how it works.  I

728
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:18,120
hear you speak,

729
00:36:18,200 --> 00:36:20,040
and when I've heard you speak
on other occasions

730
00:36:20,120 --> 00:36:21,320
and read your books, you

731
00:36:21,400 --> 00:36:24,800
also talk
about the need to reflect

732
00:36:24,880 --> 00:36:27,440
and educate ourselves about emotions,

733
00:36:27,520 --> 00:36:31,200
which is something that, probably,
my parents' generation didn't do

734
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:33,880
and that in my generation we
may try,

735
00:36:33,960 --> 00:36:36,400
perhaps, with little success, but we try.

736
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:40,440
What is it like to learn to reflect
on your own emotions?

737
00:36:40,520 --> 00:36:41,600
What you were saying

738
00:36:41,680 --> 00:36:46,080
about how our children will
learn to reflect on things

739
00:36:46,160 --> 00:36:49,440
based on the way
we look at them.

740
00:36:49,800 --> 00:36:52,720
I believe that there has always been
emotional education

741
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:54,320
and it has always existed in families

742
00:36:54,400 --> 00:36:56,360
and, in fact, it is the place where learning takes place.

743
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:58,880
Now, also in schools,
but not in books,

744
00:36:58,960 --> 00:37:04,240
but how the teacher realizes
or doesn't realize how the kids are

745
00:37:04,320 --> 00:37:07,080
and how, more or less, he
manages to regulate his own stress,

746
00:37:07,160 --> 00:37:10,960
that we have teachers,
poor things, a little stressed too.

747
00:37:11,560 --> 00:37:14,480
This is the true school
of emotions, what

748
00:37:14,560 --> 00:37:16,960
happens in relationships
with the people

749
00:37:17,040 --> 00:37:20,360
around you
while you are growing up.

750
00:37:21,400 --> 00:37:25,040
Man, if we know how
this emotional issue works

751
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:28,880
and learn a little about
the rules of the brain's game,

752
00:37:29,880 --> 00:37:32,480
maybe we can realize things

753
00:37:32,560 --> 00:37:36,080
that we may have been dragging
from our families,

754
00:37:36,440 --> 00:37:40,240
because in each family
these things are managed in a different way.

755
00:37:40,560 --> 00:37:41,800
An example is hugs.

756
00:37:41,880 --> 00:37:44,800
There are families that define themselves as:
"We don't hug,"

757
00:37:44,880 --> 00:37:46,400
as if they were a tribe.

758
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:51,000
And some of these people,
curiously enough, when they have children,

759
00:37:51,960 --> 00:37:53,400
do dare to hug them because,

760
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:56,040
somehow,
they sense that perhaps they themselves

761
00:37:56,120 --> 00:37:57,200
don't feel very comfortable,

762
00:37:57,280 --> 00:37:59,760
but their children
do need it.

763
00:37:59,840 --> 00:38:03,600
And thanks to their children, they
get used to giving

764
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:04,480
and receiving hugs,

765
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:08,480
which is something very useful
for regulating our emotions.

766
00:38:08,560 --> 00:38:10,960
They have created a tribe:
the hug tribe.

767
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:14,240
Yes.  They change a little

768
00:38:14,320 --> 00:38:16,680
to a new way of working.  We

769
00:38:17,080 --> 00:38:20,080
can also learn
other ways of functioning,

770
00:38:20,160 --> 00:38:21,160
but it's complicated

771
00:38:21,240 --> 00:38:24,880
because parents also have
our stories, right?

772
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:28,320
And also, sometimes,
we haven't grown in security.  That's why

773
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:32,560
I believe that the best thing we
can do for our children

774
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:36,120
is to work to grow
our own security,

775
00:38:36,200 --> 00:38:39,280
rather than learning 50 techniques

776
00:38:39,880 --> 00:38:43,480
or reading 50 books on how to be parents,

777
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:45,400
because that
sometimes stresses us out more,

778
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:49,800
otherwise how can we
feel more secure.

779
00:38:50,280 --> 00:38:52,240
Let's go back to the same point:

780
00:38:52,680 --> 00:38:57,560
less looking at the environment and more
working on ourselves.

781
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:59,520
But, look, you mentioned something

782
00:38:59,880 --> 00:39:02,800
that sometimes
creeps in from families,

783
00:39:02,880 --> 00:39:05,840
and you also mentioned,
at another point in the conversation,

784
00:39:05,920 --> 00:39:07,480
traumatic situations.

785
00:39:07,560 --> 00:39:10,360
These
truly traumatic situations,

786
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:14,280
that is, which mark
a turning point in some lives, do they

787
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:16,880
have a solution?

788
00:39:17,720 --> 00:39:19,200
Yes, of course.

789
00:39:19,280 --> 00:39:20,360
This is what you dedicate yourself to.

790
00:39:20,440 --> 00:39:22,960
Otherwise,
my profession would be meaningless.  Do

791
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:26,840
wounds heal?
Yes, of course they heal.

792
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:30,840
Sometimes, alone,
most of the time, luckily.

793
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:35,520
Emotional wounds also
heal over time.

794
00:39:35,600 --> 00:39:37,640
And other times not,
emotional wounds...

795
00:39:37,720 --> 00:39:40,280
But, since they are not visible,
we think we can pretend they are

796
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:42,800
not there and cover them up,

797
00:39:42,880 --> 00:39:46,360
and sometimes we need to help
a little to heal them.

798
00:39:47,920 --> 00:39:52,440
Sometimes, not only do we not help them
heal, but we scratch, we

799
00:39:53,120 --> 00:39:54,800
rub salt in the wounds, we get

800
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:57,600
angry with ourselves
for being there...

801
00:39:57,920 --> 00:39:59,720
And then it gets more complicated.

802
00:39:59,800 --> 00:40:02,240
But there are two issues.

803
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:06,120
One is, if something happens to me,
the impact that this could have on me.

804
00:40:06,680 --> 00:40:10,160
If this is not resolved,
it can have a

805
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:13,200
very significant influence, sometimes lasting for years,

806
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:16,960
and it can be resolved,
regardless of how much time passes.

807
00:40:17,640 --> 00:40:22,360
But when we work, we
not only take this into account,

808
00:40:22,440 --> 00:40:26,520
but also
the person's attachment style.

809
00:40:26,600 --> 00:40:29,320
And attachment style
means how it works with

810
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:31,880
respect to the issue of security.

811
00:40:32,920 --> 00:40:36,560
If a person has a secure attachment, they
are more able to reflect,

812
00:40:36,640 --> 00:40:38,360
they are more able to trust, they

813
00:40:38,880 --> 00:40:42,120
are more able to learn from experience,

814
00:40:42,200 --> 00:40:44,560
and they are more able to establish relationships.

815
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:47,880
We talk a lot about one's things,

816
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:51,880
but security
cannot be learned in isolation.  We must

818
00:40:55,640 --> 00:40:57,720
are times when we wear it
at home

819
00:40:57,800 --> 00:41:02,040
because we grew up with parents
who were quite confident people.

820
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:06,160
Later we will talk about the difference
between confident and convinced,

821
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:07,600
but so as not to lose the thread...

822
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:10,360
And other times, perhaps,

823
00:41:10,440 --> 00:41:13,200
our parents
were not confident people

824
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:15,800
and could not teach us
a language they did not know,

825
00:41:15,880 --> 00:41:18,360
but we can learn it later.

826
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:23,520
And this security will also help us
resolve these traumas,

827
00:41:23,600 --> 00:41:27,880
and even when
potentially traumatic things happen to us,

828
00:41:28,480 --> 00:41:31,800
we will be able to bear it better, we

829
00:41:31,880 --> 00:41:34,800
will be able to face
these kinds of experiences better.

830
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:37,800
It's a combination of both.

831
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:40,560
If something happens to a child,
the parents are a cushion.

832
00:41:42,800 --> 00:41:46,360
In some families,
unfortunately, a mattress with spikes,

833
00:41:46,440 --> 00:41:48,960
and in others, a soft mattress,

834
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:52,360
and we must try to be a soft mattress

835
00:41:52,440 --> 00:41:54,160
so that, when things happen to them,

836
00:41:54,240 --> 00:41:57,360
these things
don't affect them as much.

837
00:41:57,680 --> 00:42:00,720
But things happen
and we need to help them deal with them.

838
00:42:00,800 --> 00:42:02,640
But you said you were going to explain to me

839
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:05,480
the difference between sure and convinced,

840
00:42:05,560 --> 00:42:08,160
and I won't get up from my chair
until you explain it to me.

841
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:13,560
Sometimes, we think that a confident person
is someone who says things like that.  Do

842
00:42:13,640 --> 00:42:16,160
you think Donald Trump
is a safe person?

843
00:42:17,360 --> 00:42:18,720
Yes, it seems so.  It

844
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:22,840
seems so, but it wouldn't be
the definition of a confident person at all.

845
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:26,280
He's an arrogant, dogmatic person, right?

846
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:28,800
A confident person
is a person who doubts.

847
00:42:29,720 --> 00:42:33,120
Not that he doubts everything, we are
not usually good at extremes,

848
00:42:33,640 --> 00:42:34,640
but he is a person who doubts

849
00:42:34,720 --> 00:42:36,800
because, if he doesn't,
he is a person who doesn't learn.

850
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:41,600
And, if we don't learn,
we are always in the same place,

851
00:42:41,680 --> 00:42:46,000
and that is not security, it is entrenching ourselves
in my convictions.

852
00:42:47,160 --> 00:42:50,000
Many times,
very convinced people

853
00:42:50,080 --> 00:42:52,120
are deeply insecure people

854
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:54,400
who say things in capital letters

855
00:42:54,800 --> 00:42:57,480
so that others
don't shake them.

856
00:42:58,680 --> 00:42:59,480
How good.

857
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:04,480
We see what we need to do to learn
to analyze these emotions,

858
00:43:04,560 --> 00:43:06,520
but what happens if we are not able

859
00:43:06,600 --> 00:43:08,960
to analyze
our own emotions?

860
00:43:09,040 --> 00:43:11,760
What dangers exist,
if any?

861
00:43:11,840 --> 00:43:14,720
The entire emotional mechanism is there

862
00:43:14,800 --> 00:43:17,560
so that we know
what things mean,

863
00:43:18,080 --> 00:43:19,400
what we need,

864
00:43:19,480 --> 00:43:23,320
and, furthermore, they must lead us to
seek what we need.

865
00:43:23,400 --> 00:43:25,720
Emotions
really have this function,

866
00:43:25,800 --> 00:43:27,600
each one their own.

867
00:43:28,240 --> 00:43:30,760
If we
don't follow the rules of the game,

868
00:43:30,840 --> 00:43:33,720
so to speak,
trying to avoid...

869
00:43:34,840 --> 00:43:37,680
I can't help but feel afraid, it

870
00:43:37,760 --> 00:43:39,920
would be like saying:
"I want to avoid having lungs."

871
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:41,560
Well, I can't.

872
00:43:41,840 --> 00:43:44,640
Every emotion has a function,
they all have meaning.

873
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:47,840
So, trying to avoid them,
trying to control them...

874
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:49,440
If I try to control an emotion

875
00:43:49,520 --> 00:43:51,840
it's like trying to hold a cat very tightly.

876
00:43:53,280 --> 00:43:54,280
Things will happen,

877
00:43:54,360 --> 00:43:57,200
but they probably
won't be very convenient.  There

878
00:43:57,280 --> 00:43:59,360
are times when we don't even realize

879
00:43:59,440 --> 00:44:01,040
